//! `call_f64x2_u32x2_ret`, `call_u64_f64_u32x2`); the `f1` argument that the
//! export prints as a leading `undefined8` is modelled as an explicit `f64`.
//!
//! | address range | count | role |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `0x800E20A0` | 1 | notification pose push with the ten-entry table |
//! | `0x800E28A0` | 1 | packed pair of the two corrected display metrics |
//! | `0x800E2DE0..0x800E3160` | 4 | three-float and two-float mesh strip decoders |
//! | `0x800E3BA0` | 1 | sprite slot reset and mask rebuild |
//! | `0x800E6090..0x800E63A0` | 2 | effect frame accumulation and window clamp |
//! | `0x800EEAE0` | 1 | resource-name table scan |
//! | `0x800EEFF0` | 1 | two-slot variant rebuild |
//! | `0x800EF570` | 1 | effect record selector and audio toggle |
//! | `0x800EF770..0x800EF940` | 3 | keyed track rebuild and the two frame snaps |
//!
//! Seven entries remained untranslated; every one of them was read in full and
//! the reason is recorded in `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE103_DSYS.md`.
//!
//! Wave 107 closed 4 of those 7: the glyph emitters that the text-stream
//! dispatcher `0x800E6290` calls. The blocker was a misreading, not the byte
//! count. Every one of those exports opens with `psq_st`/`psq_l` pairs of
//! `f21..f31`; those are the **non-volatile register saves**, not arguments, so
//! the dead `(float)in_fNN` blocks can be dropped exactly like the `ldexpf`
//! saves noted in Wave 97. `0x800E5DD0`'s varying `0x802DCC4C` arity is settled
//! the same way: `work/dol/main.elf` at `0x800E5F30` sets only `f1` before the
//! `bl`, so the extra printed doubles are the live `f2..f6` registers.
//!
//! | address range | count | role |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `0x800E5820` | 1 | glyph quad emit and `+0x48` virtual step |
//! | `0x800E5AD0` | 1 | vertical window push and two run re-measures |
//! | `0x800E5DD0` | 1 | text cell corner fit and quad draw |
//! | `0x800E6680` | 1 | longer-of-two run advance |
//!
//! Three entries remained untranslated (`0x800E0540`, `0x800E9130`,
//! `0x800EC3B0`); each was re-read in Wave 107 and the reason is recorded in
//! `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE107_DSYS.md`.
//!
//! Wave 110 closed those last three. The Wave 107 note on `0x800E9130` was a
//! misattribution: the eight colour bytes it described belong to `0x800EC3B0`,
//! and `0x800E9130` contains no `stb` at all (`work/dol/main.elf`,
//! `0x800E9130..0x800E9538`). `0x800E0540` is a 0x6A0-byte header plus three
//! pairs of 0x980-byte record copies, and `0x800EC3B0`'s colour chain resolves
//! to two explicit four-byte words at `+0xD1CC`/`+0xD21C`.
//!
//! | address range | count | role |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `0x800E0540` | 1 | ribbon header and six-record marshal |
//! | `0x800E9130` | 1 | ribbon rail generator over the node chain |
//! | `0x800EC3B0` | 1 | ribbon emitter respawn, clamp and colour ramp |
//!
//! No `0x800E0000` bucket entry remains untranslated.
//!
//! ## Why it exists
//!
//! `engine::startup::game_startup` is `0x800E48D0(); 0x800E47A0(0);`. This is
//! therefore the *first* thing the game thread does, before the first frame.
//! It turns the OS arena into the engine's heap hierarchy and starts the
//! subsystems that `0x800E4E40` relies on.
//!
//! ## Heap model (observed calls, inferred roles)
//!
//! The `dSys_c` object is the initialized-data block at `0x80371AA0` (inside
//! the BSS window). Its first word is the vtable (standard NW4R layout); the
//! fields used here are:
//!
//! | field | use in `0x800E48D0` |
//! |---|---|
//! | `+0x00` | vtable pointer; slot `+0x38` is called once as an initializer |
//! | `+0x18` | second argument of the `RootHeapMEM1` creation call |
//! | `+0x1C` | second argument of the `RootHeapMEM2` creation call |
//! | `+0x44` | display info read by `0x800E8AB0` |
//! | `+0x4C` | child manager used by the step helpers |
//!
//! `// inferred:` `+0x18` / `+0x1C` are the two root-heap sizes. The strings
//! prove the heaps' identity; the field semantics are inferred from position,
//! because the callee body drops its first two arguments (see **Unknowns**).
//!
//! ```text
//! dSys_c (0x80371AA0)
//!   +-- RootHeapMEM1  (name at 0x8031B050)   size from +0x18
//!   +-- RootHeapMEM2  (name at 0x8031B068)   size from +0x1C
//!   |
//!   +-- MEM1 sub-heaps: 0x500000 (0x8016EB20), 0x980000 (heap slot 1)
//!   +-- MEM2 sub-heaps: 0x700000 (heap slot 2), 0x1200000, 0x1000
//!   +-- MEM2 lazy block: 0x400000 (only if the slot is still null)
//!   +-- MEM2 pool:      0x1C2000, element size 4
//! ```
//!
//! Heap handles live in the three-word array at `0x80377C48`:
//! `+0x00` is the **default/current** heap, `+0x04` is the 0x980000 MEM1
//! sub-heap, `+0x08` is the 0x700000 MEM2 sub-heap. `0x8016E9F0` installs a
//! newly created sub-heap into its slot and mirrors it into slot 0 when it is
//! the selected default (`r13 - 0x61F0` = `0x80429490`).
//!
//! ## Bring-up order
//!
//! 1. remember the current thread, install the `dSys_c` object, run vtable
//!    slot `+0x38`;
//! 2. create the two root heaps (`0x8016E500`);
//! 3. read the display metrics (`0x800E8AB0`);
//! 4. create the MEM1/MEM2 sub-heaps (`0x8016EB20`, `0x8016EAA0`,
//!    `0x8016EAB0`, `0x8016EAC0`, `0x8016EAF0`);
//! 5. switch the current allocator to the default heap (`0x802B9170`), build
//!    the 0x38-byte object and the 0x2A0000-byte subsystem (`0x802D3490`);
//! 6. renderer (`0x8016F1F0`), two 0x14-byte services (`0x800B58E0`,
//!    `0x800DC430`), four slots (`0x801EE150`), the four views (`0x8016F220`);
//! 7. resource/scene bring-up (`0x800B6460`, `0x800B0B50`, `0x800B0DB0`);
//! 8. layout block (lazy 0x400000), main game object (`0x801073B0`);
//! 9. frame counters reset (`0x8015FE40`), worker thread (`0x800CFAB0`),
//!    display and layout objects (`0x801092F0`, `0x80107EC0`);
//! 10. effect/particle storage (`0x80069720`), the 1 MiB/2.5 MiB blocks
//!     ([`fn_800e45f0`]), the 0x1C2000 pool (`0x801021D0`);
//! 11. mark both root heaps (`+0x1C |= 1`), reset the scene transition
//!     (`0x800E1DB0`), publish the byte `1` and the `0xFF` word
//!     ([`fn_800e4830`], [`fn_800e4850`]), run the empty `0x80006790`.
//!
//! ## Dependencies
//!
//! * [`crate::platform`] - big-endian volatile access and indirect calls.
//! * No other lane's module is required. Every callee is called by absolute
//!   address because it belongs to a subsystem outside this file.
//!
//! ## Start here
//!
//! [`fn_800e48d0`] reads top to bottom in the original order. The helpers are
//! tiny and can be read afterwards; [`fn_800e46e0`] is the best introduction
//! to the `dSys` object's child layout, because it shows both the child
//! function-pointer table and the object's own vtable.
//!
//! ## Glossary
//!
//! * **r13 / SDA** - the PowerPC small-data base register. `0x800041C0` sets
//!   `r13 = 0x8042F680`; `r13 - 0x55F4` is `0x8042A08C`.
//! * **MEM1 / MEM2** - the Wii's 24 MiB main RAM and the extra 64 MiB.
//! * **root heap** - the engine's top-level allocator, named by the two
//!   `dSys_c::RootHeapMEM1/MEM2` strings.
//! * **NW4R** - Nintendo's Wii runtime library; its objects put the vtable in
//!   the first word.
//! * **`dSys_c`** - the engine root object; the name comes from the literal
//!   strings passed to the root-heap calls.
//!
//! ## Status
//!
//! All 402 functions in this file are represented (397 ledgered plus five
//! untabled bodies): the 18 `dSys` start-up
//! bodies above, the 30 Wave 75 bodies from the three adjacent clusters, the 28
//! Wave 89 bodies that closed the contiguous runs, the 26 Wave 93 bodies, the 25
//! Wave 97 bodies, the 22 Wave 103/107/110 bodies (15 + 4 + 3), the 61 Wave 215
//! bodies, the 60 Wave 216 bodies, the 60 Wave 217 bodies, the 68 Wave 218
//! bodies, and the 2 + 2 untabled bodies of Waves 226 and 228. With Wave 110
//! the `0x800E0000` bucket has no entries left untranslated; the later passes
//! work the adjacent `0x800F0000` bucket.
//! `fn_800e48d0` is `partial`:
//! its control flow and every call argument are translated, but the callees
//! are opaque, so their effects are recorded, not reconstructed. The helpers
//! are `translated`. Confidence is `high` for control flow and `medium` for
//! the inferred subsystem roles.

use crate::game::bases::interface_call;
use crate::platform;

/// Small-data base register `r13` for main-`DOL` code (`0x8042F680`).
const SDA_BASE: usize = 0x8042_F680;

/// Second small-data base `r2` (`0x80433080`), used for float constants.
///
/// Observed: `0x800041C0` sets `r2 = 0x80433080`. The two floats read below
/// (`r2 - 0x63A8`, `r2 - 0x63A4`) are resolved against it.
const R2_BASE: usize = 0x8043_3080;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SDA globals touched by this module
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `r13 - 0x55F8` = `0x8042A088`: the thread that ran `0x800E48D0`.
///
/// Set from `0x801B4B30()` (`OSGetCurrentThread`, a load from `0x800000E4`).
const G_CURRENT_THREAD: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x55F8; // 0x8042A088

/// `r13 - 0x55F4` = `0x8042A08C`: pointer to the `dSys_c` object.
///
/// `0x800E48D0` stores `0x80371AA0` here; every helper in this module reads
/// it back instead of using the absolute address directly.
const G_D_SYS: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x55F4; // 0x8042A08C

/// `r13 - 0x4838` = `0x8042AE48`: the allocator used by `0x8016E500` when its
/// handle lookup returns null.
///
/// `0x800E48D0` points it at the same `dSys_c` object.
const G_ALLOCATOR: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4838; // 0x8042AE48

/// `r13 - 0x4870` = `0x8042AE10`: the **current allocator** global.
///
/// `0x802B9170(new)` swaps this word and returns the old value; `0x800E48D0`
/// uses that to install the default heap for the start-up allocations and then
/// to restore the previous value.
const G_CURRENT_ALLOCATOR: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x4870; // 0x8042AE10

/// `r13 - 0x55F0` = `0x8042A090`: handle of the `RootHeapMEM1` heap.
const G_ROOT_HEAP_MEM1: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x55F0; // 0x8042A090

/// `r13 - 0x55EC` = `0x8042A094`: handle of the `RootHeapMEM2` heap.
const G_ROOT_HEAP_MEM2: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x55EC; // 0x8042A094

/// `r13 - 0x55E8` = `0x8042A098`: lazy 0x400000-byte MEM2 block (layout/
/// resource data), created only when still null.
const G_LAYOUT_BLOCK: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x55E8; // 0x8042A098

/// `r13 - 0x5600` = `0x8042A080`: result of the 1 MiB allocation in
/// [`fn_800e45f0`].
const G_BLOCK_1M: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x5600; // 0x8042A080

/// `r13 - 0x55FC` = `0x8042A084`: result of the 2.5 MiB allocation in
/// [`fn_800e45f0`].
const G_BLOCK_25M: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x55FC; // 0x8042A084

/// `r13 - 0x522C` = `0x8042A454`: result of `0x8016EB20(0x500000, mem1)`.
///
/// `// unknown:` the consumer of this handle.
const G_BLOCK_5M: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x522C; // 0x8042A454

/// `r13 - 0x5230` = `0x8042A450`: the 0x1000-byte MEM2 block.
///
/// Passed to `0x8008F110` together with [`G_BLOCK_18M`] and the current
/// thread's priority minus one.
const G_BLOCK_4K: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x5230; // 0x8042A450

/// `r13 - 0x5234` = `0x8042A44C`: the 0x1200000-byte MEM2 block.
const G_BLOCK_18M: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x5234; // 0x8042A44C

/// `r13 - 0x5218` = `0x8042A468`: one of the four view objects at
/// `0x80377C88..0x80377C94`, selected by `0x8016F220`.
///
/// `0x800E48D0` calls virtual slot `+8` on it with two float arguments after
/// `0x8016F220`.
const G_VIEW_OBJECT: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x5218; // 0x8042A468

/// `0x80371AA0`: the `dSys_c` object itself, in the initialized-data area
/// embedded in BSS.
///
/// The two root-heap names prove the class; its first word is the vtable.
const D_SYS_OBJECT: usize = 0x8037_1AA0;

/// `0x8031B050`: literal C string `"dSys_c::RootHeapMEM1"`.
const ROOT_HEAP_MEM1_NAME: usize = 0x8031_B050;

/// `0x8031B068`: literal C string `"dSys_c::RootHeapMEM2"`.
const ROOT_HEAP_MEM2_NAME: usize = 0x8031_B068;

/// `0x80377C48`: three-word heap-handle array.
///
/// `+0x00` default/current heap, `+0x04` 0x980000 MEM1 sub-heap, `+0x08`
/// 0x700000 MEM2 sub-heap. See `0x8016E9F0`, which writes the slots.
const G_HEAP_ARRAY: usize = 0x8037_7C48;

/// `0x80429490`: `r13 - 0x61F0`, the "selected default heap" byte.
///
/// `// inferred:` `0x8016E9F0` copies a new sub-heap into slot 0 when its index
/// equals this byte, so it selects the default heap between MEM1 (1) and MEM2
/// (2).
const G_DEFAULT_HEAP_INDEX: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x61F0; // 0x80429490

/// `r2 - 0x63A8` = `0x8042CCD8`: first float argument of the view call.
///
/// The value lives in `.data` and is not in the Ghidra export; it is read at
/// run time so no constant is invented.
const F_VIEW_ARG_1: usize = R2_BASE - 0x63A8; // 0x8042CCD8

/// `r2 - 0x63A4` = `0x8042CCDC`: second float argument of the view call.
const F_VIEW_ARG_2: usize = R2_BASE - 0x63A4; // 0x8042CCDC

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Callees outside this module
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `0x801B4B30()` - `OSGetCurrentThread`; returns `*(u32*)0x800000E4`.
const FN_801B4B30: usize = 0x801B_4B30;

/// `0x801B5F70(thread)` - returns the `OSThread` base priority at `+0x2D4`.
const FN_801B5F70: usize = 0x801B_5F70;

/// `0x8016E500(...)` - five-argument allocator/heap constructor.
///
/// Ghidra prints `void`, but every call site consumes `r3`; the body ends in a
/// tail call to `0x802DCDBC`, which returns the new object. Modelled as
/// `-> u32`.
///
/// `// unknown:` which of the first two arguments is the byte size. The body
/// drops both and uses the fourth argument (`0x20`) as an internal allocation
/// size, so the decompiled signature is not trustworthy. The call sites below
/// pass the arguments exactly as Ghidra shows them.
const FN_8016E500: usize = 0x8016_E500;

/// `0x8016E630(...)` - same shape as [`FN_8016E500`], different final
/// allocator (Ghidra `FUN_8016E7D0` instead of `0x8016E610`).
const FN_8016E630: usize = 0x8016_E630;

/// `0x8016EB20(size, heap)` - creates a block and stores it at
/// [`G_BLOCK_5M`].
const FN_8016EB20: usize = 0x8016_EB20;

/// `0x8016EAA0(size, heap)` - `0x8016E9F0(1, size, heap)`: installs the
